Z||000004||reddog||12-15-2001||11:03 PM||||Mark I believe he is talking about the Oil Pressure Sending unit! Yes your oil pressure should fluctuate a little depending on engine temperature and rpm. A sending unit costs about $35.00 if my memory is correct. Try a can of connector cleaner it worked on my GT for a little while. The hunting idle is a whole different ball of wax. If you do a search on the topic I think you'll find that most forum members have or have had the same problem at one time or another. It could be a whole $hit load of things causing it. Some of the most common causes are;<P>Idle Air control valve<BR>Throttle Position sensor<BR>Intake vacuum leak<BR>Cracked vacuum lines/hoses<P>Some member have also suggested the following :<P>Dirty TB around throttle plate or IAC passage<BR>EGR valve or solenoid<BR>Faulty Ignition system<BR>ECM  farked-up<BR>fuel pump<P>Someone even suggested that it could be a bad Battery or Alternator!!! Good luck finding the cause! Start with the easy things first get a can of Intake cleaner and spray it around all the vacuum lines and intake gaskets. If the idle changes drastically when sprayed then you have found the problem.However it is a real SOB getting to and changing the lines/hoses. Next try taking out the IAC valve and cleaning the housing it seats. If that doesn't work, replace the IAC and then go on to the TPS. There is a lot more things to check. Like I said before just do a search and you should have plenty of info on what else to do.Hope you find it. Does the surge only occur inpark& neutral? Does it surge while you are driving? <P><P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||tgowens||12-16-2001||12:17 AM||||Mark, first, there is nothing wrong with 60psi, mine runs as high as 70 and drops to 40 at idle - I have checked it manually so I would know where it should read. You will find that, on the Fiero, the Oil Pressure Sending Unit is not the most of reliable things. It is located on the right side of the engine just to the right of the oil dip stick and behind the EGR Solenoid. There are three wires in the connector on the top of the sending unit. Due to its vertical position it is possible for moisture to get under the plug which can give faulty readings. <P>Now, here is the other part -- remove the sending unit and connect a guage to the line to check your pressure manually. Reason? You will also find that the guages on the Fiero are the least reliable. I installed four before I found one that read proper oil pressure.<P>As for the hunting idle - somewhat characteristic of a Fiero to rise and fall 100 to 200. Almost every one I have seen does this.||||reg||1
Z||000006||SLYGUY||12-16-2001||12:33 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B>As for the hunting idle - somewhat characteristic of a Fiero to rise and fall 100 to 200. Almost every one I have seen does this.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have to agree. I had the same problem and gave up. After replacing the EGR, the O2 sensor, distributor cap and rotor, spark plugs and resetting the computer a couple of times it is still there. Don't pull the pin on the grenade just yet though. Be sure to check for the 'lope' only when the temp is up. You need to be running at least 185 degrees before you can expect a great idle. <BR>My 88GT idles at 800rpm warmed up. Well, for about 10-15 seconds or so then it will burp for no reason at all and rise to 900, fall to 775 then back to 800 for another 10-20 seconds or so. <P>All cars have a personality. You found our cars'.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ViewImage.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by SLYGUY (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
